.TH SSH-SELECTOR 1 "2026-07-24" "mu2edaq-cluster-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ssh-selector \- keyboard-driven terminal UI for picking and connecting to SSH hosts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ssh-selector
[\fB\-c\fR \fIFILE\fR]
.br
.B ssh-selector
\fB\-\-version\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ssh-selector
is a Textual-based terminal UI for navigating a fleet of remote hosts
defined in a YAML configuration file. Hosts are organized into named,
searchable groups; each host may offer several login users, a fixed
SSH port, and an optional ProxyJump gateway. Selecting a host opens an
interactive SSH session, a new terminal window, or an SSH port-forward
tunnel.
.PP
While running, the right-hand panel shows connection details for the
highlighted host, active Kerberos tickets with expiry countdowns, the
local network identity, and any background tunnels that have been
opened.
.PP
If the destination host's resolved IP address falls within a locally
detected subnet, a configured ProxyJump is automatically skipped since
the gateway is not needed to reach it directly.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR
Path to a YAML configuration file. Skips config-file auto-discovery
when given.
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version number and exit.
.SH CONFIG FILE DISCOVERY
When \fB\-c\fR is not given, configuration files are searched for in
this order:
.IP 1. 4
\fB*.yaml\fR files in the current directory
.IP 2. 4
\fB*.yaml\fR files in \fB./config/\fR
.PP
If exactly one file is found it is loaded automatically. If several
are found, a selection screen lists them for you to pick from. If
none are found, run with \fB\-c\fR to point at one explicitly. See
\fIconfig/hosts.yaml.example\fR for a fully annotated example
configuration.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
.TP
.B Enter
Connect to the selected host in the current terminal.
.TP
.B n
Connect to the selected host in a new terminal window.
.TP
.B t
Open an SSH port-forward tunnel to the selected host; prompts for a
local port, a remote port, and whether to run the tunnel in the
background.
.TP
.B c
Check how many users are logged in on every host (via a non-interactive
\fBwho\fR over SSH) and display the count next to each nickname.
.TP
.B /
Focus the search field to filter the host list by nickname or hostname.
.TP
.B r
Renew the current Kerberos ticket principal via \fBkinit \-R\fR.
.TP
.B A
Show the About screen.
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.TP
.B Left / Right arrows
Switch the selected host between the two columns of a paired row.
.TP
.B Escape
Clear the search field and return focus to the host list, or dismiss
the active modal; quits if pressed with no search text and no modal
open.
.PP
In the tunnels panel, \fBk\fR or \fBDelete\fR terminates the
highlighted background tunnel.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I config/hosts.yaml.example
Fully annotated example host configuration.
.TP
.I ./*.yaml, ./config/*.yaml
Auto-discovered configuration files (see CONFIG FILE DISCOVERY above).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal exit, including the user quitting the host selector or the
config-file picker.
.TP
.B 1
No configuration file was found (or the one given with \fB\-c\fR does
not exist), the configuration failed to parse, or it defines no hosts.
.SH KERBEROS SUPPORT
Kerberos ticket support relies on \fBklist\fR and \fBkinit\fR being
available on \fBPATH\fR. Support on Windows is limited: it works within
WSL and from PowerShell when Kerberos is configured, but MIT Kerberos
for Windows has compatibility issues with some terminal emulators.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lan-scan (1)
